\section{Styling \& Customising}<<CMD_495>>

\subsection{Global Styling}

If you want to change the properties of an entire circuit such that all the typically black elements are a different colour, and the backgrounds of cells are another, you can supply the quantikz command with two keys: <<CODE_392>> and <<CODE_393>>. The second of these works well alongside the <<CODE_394>> command for making the rest of the page a particular colour.
\begin{Code}
\begin{quantikz}[color=blue,background color=yellow]
\lstick{\ket{\psi}} & \gate{H} & \gate{X} & \meter{}
\end{quantikz}
\end{Code}

There are two further keys that change styles globally: `thin lines' to make the lines thin, more in keeping with what QCircuit produced, and `transparent', should you want the background of all the gates to be transparent:
\begin{Code}
\begin{quantikz}[thin lines,transparent]
& \ctrl{1} & & \ctrl{1} & & \\
& \targ{} & \gate{R_z(-\theta/2)} & \targ{} & \gate{R_z(\theta/2)} & \meter{}
\end{quantikz}
\end{Code}

Global properties that affect all circuit elements of a given type can be affected through <<CODE_395>>.
\begin{Code}
\tikzset{
 operator/.append style={fill=red!20},
 my label/.append style={above right,xshift=0.3cm},
 phase label/.append style={label position=above}
}
\begin{quantikz}
& \gate{H} & \phase{\beta} & \gate{H} & \meter{\ket{\pm}}
\end{quantikz}
\end{Code}
The global styles are:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|c}
Style Name & Affected Command(s)  \\
\hline
operator & <<CODE_396>>  \\
meter & <<CODE_397>> \\
slice & <<CODE_398>> \\
wave & <<CODE_399>> \\
leftinternal & <<CODE_400>> \\
rightinternal   & <<CODE_401>> \\
dm & left braces (<<CODE_402>>,<<CODE_403>>) \\
dd & right braces (<<CODE_404>>,<<CODE_405>>) \\
phase & <<CODE_406>>, <<CODE_407>>, <<CODE_408>>, <<CODE_409>> \\
circlewc & <<CODE_410>> \\
crossx2 & <<CODE_411>>,<<CODE_412>> \\
my label & measurement bases in <<CODE_413>> \\
phase label & phases in <<CODE_414>> \\
gg label & main gate label in <<CODE_415>> \\
group label & label in <<CODE_416>>
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Per-Gate Styling}

Individual gates can be modified using optional arguments of the calling function. 
\begin{Code}
\begin{quantikz}
& \gate[style={fill=red!20},label style=cyan]{H} & \phase[style={green},label style={label position=above}]{\beta} & \gate{H} & & \meter[style={draw=blue}]{\ket{\pm}}
\end{quantikz}
\end{Code}
The specific syntax varies a little depending on the type of gate. See the commands list for the specific cases.
