If you want to input several styling parameters with one of the keys, just group them together in a set of curly braces, \{\}. Typical styling parameters include <<CODE_417>> specifying line colour, <<CODE_418>>, specifying fill colour, <<CODE_419>> and <<CODE_420>> specifying horizontal and vertical margins respectively, <<CODE_421>> and <<CODE_422>> for adjusting horizontal and vertical positioning. Beyond that, the \href{http://mirrors.ctan.org/graphics/pgf/base/doc/pgfmanual.pdf}{tikz manual} is your friend!

This styling is really quite flexible, as we can override the default shapes with anything that we want:
\begin{Code}
\tikzset{
 noisy/.style={starburst,fill=yellow,draw=red,line width=2pt,inner xsep=-4pt,inner ysep=-5pt}
}
\begin{quantikz}[row sep=0.3cm,column sep=0.3cm,wire types={q,q,q,n,n}]<<COMMENT_129>>
& \gate{H} & \ctrl{2}& & \gate[3,style={noisy},label style=cyan]{\text{noise}} & \ctrl{3} &  & & \\
\lstick{\ket{0}} & & \targ{} & & & & \ctrl{3} & & \\
\lstick{\ket{0}} & & \targ{}& & & & & \ctrl{2} & \\
&&&&\lstick{\ket{0}} & \targ{}\setwiretype{q} & \targ{} & & \meter{} \\
&&&&\lstick{\ket{0}} & \setwiretype{q} & \targ{} & \targ{} & \meter{} 
\end{quantikz}
\end{Code}
